  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nPer a recent subjective surface analyses, a 1000 mb surface low\npressure system is moving across southern Manitoba well to the \nnorth of a surface high pressure system parked along the \nAtlantic coast. A cold front extends southward from the low \npressure system through northern Minnesota and across the \ncentral Plains. Ahead of the front, a broad ribbon of somewhat \nunfocused low-level isentropic ascent is supporting areas of \nshowers and thunderstorms from Nebraska northeastward into \ncentral Wisconsin. A pocket of relative dry air is present \nsoutheast of the axis of isentropic ascent, which is leading to \na pleasant day across the Lower Great Lakes. \n\nThis afternoon and evening, the upper-level wave currently \nsupporting showers and thunderstorms across eastern Nebraska \nwill shift eastward into Iowa and support the \nexpansion/invigoration of thunderstorms. After sunset, radar \ndata to our west may look rather intimidating with what may be \nseveral clusters of strong to severe storms moving toward the \nMississippi River. Thankfully, the environment east of the river\nwill be hostile for thunderstorms to maintain their intensity, \nso the expectation remains for thunderstorm clusters to \ngradually weaken as they traverse eastward into northern \nIllinois through the night. Peak coverage of activity through \ndaybreak should be mainly along and north of I-88 and west of \nI-39. \n\nWith time, the axis of remnant showers will drift southward \nthrough the area from sunrise through early afternoon on \nWednesday. Coverage should then decrease with time from north to\nsouth through the afternoon as a cold front works across the \narea. Redevelopment of thunderstorms along the front appears to \nbe a largely unfavored scenario given limited opportunities for \ndestabilization tomorrow. In all, the next 24 hours doesn't look\nlike a complete washout across our area with most areas favored\nto get less than 0.5\" of an inch of total rainfall. \n\nTemperatures tomorrow will be modulated by remnant showers and \ncloud cover. While there are some prospects for clearing from \nnorth to south through the afternoon, did opt to cut back NBM \ntemperatures to largely advertise highs around 80 degrees. \nTomorrow night, a few waves propagating along the front should \nallow for additional waves of showers and a few storms primarily\nsouth of our area. Did cut back on NBM PoPs to paint low-end \nchances (20-40%) for showers near the US-24 corridor through \ndaybreak Thursday. \n\nA surface high pressure system will slide through the region \nThursday and Friday leading to quiescent conditions. Both days \nwill be characterized by mostly sunny skies, light winds, and \nhighs around 80 degrees. A series of upper-level waves may \nprovide the opportunity for a few windows for precipitation this\nweekend into early next week. \n\nBorchardt",
